WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:06.000
This is the Technical Difficulties,
we're playing Citation Needed.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.440
Joining me today, he reads books y'know,

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:08.680
-it's Chris Joel.
-In space.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:11.800
Everybody's favourite Gary Brannan,
Gary Brannan.

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:13.520
Nice to see you, to see you...

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:14.560
Nice!

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:18.520
And the bounciest man on the internet,
Matt Gray.

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:20.040
-You planned that?
-Yeah.

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:22.760
I planned nothing.

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:27.640
In front of me I've got an article from
Wikipedia and these folks can't see it.

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:29.880
Every fact they get right is a point
and a ding,

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:32.320
and there's a special prize for
particularly good answers, which is...

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:38.760
And today we are talking about
Juan Pujol Garcia.

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:41.200
Juan Pujol, is there are two of them,
is he on his own?

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:43.040
It's a good job this isn't QI, Gary,

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:45.600
because at that part large klaxons would be
going off with that joke.

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:47.600
Just saying t*** repeatedly.

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:51.040
Can I guess that this person is from a
Spanish or Portuguese speaking country?

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:53.040
Er, yeah, Spain.

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:55.520
-Hey!
-I don't know very much about Spain.

00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:58.200
It's funny you say
that you don't know much about Spain.

00:00:58.200 --> 00:00:59.160
Did he neither?

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:02.880
He didn't know much about England and that
became important during part of his life.

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:05.840
Did he just end up here and was like
'S***, what's this?'

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:08.080
Well, I'm going to have a run at this one
and miss it,

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:10.560
but was he some kind of captain
of the Spanish Armada?

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:13.280
Ooh, no,
you're far, far, far too early for that.

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:15.680
Was he not the captain of any kind of
Spanish Armada?

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:17.960
Yes, but you're not getting a point
for not saying he's not something.

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:21.120
Was he not the first man
to walk on the moon, is true.

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:24.440
If he was too early for an armada
he's going to be too early for the moon.

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:26.520
No, he's too … he's too late for an armada.

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:28.800
-Too late for an armada.
-The armada was too early for him?

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:31.160
-Yes. The armada left, he hadn't been born yet.
-He missed the bus.

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:32.520
It was centuries, it is centuries later.

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:36.120
"Did I miss the train
or did the train miss me?" he cries.

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:41.960
Yeah, he was centuries late for the armada,
he was born in 1912, died in 1988.

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:44.080
Aha, that doesn't help much.

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:46.560
It doesn’t, but he had a code name.

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:48.680
-Did he now?
-Given those dates,

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:50.560
why might you want a code name?

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:52.080
-Spies.
-Yes.

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:53.680
-Is this to do with the government of Fran...?
-Oh!

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:59.280
Was he a spy placed in England but
he just didn't know anything about the place?

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:00.600
So was he s*** spy?

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:05.510
No, he was a spy placed in England to look
after sheep and therefore:

00:02:05.510 --> 00:02:06.820
was a shepherd-spy!

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:12.840
[Subtitler's note: Gary's joke is a pun on "shepherd's pie",
but for the first time in the show's history,
this pun is entirely untranslatable to text.]

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:17.640
That's not olden, that's golden.

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:25.040
Two people in the audience just going,
"No. No, we're not rewarding that."

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:30.280
No, he was … he was an excellent spy,
but for which side?

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:31.680
And we're talking World War 2 here.

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:35.400
-Both.
-Yes, absolutely right, he was a double agent.

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:37.400
Is he involved
in the 'man who never was' thing?

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:39.360
No, this was not Operation Mincemeat.

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:40.480
-Right.
-This was not that.

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:43.720
Because there was someone in that who was
sending information both ways, wasn't there?

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:48.480
This was … this was a man who received
both an Iron Cross from Germany

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:50.040
and an MBE from Britain.

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:52.480
How can that make him a good spy?
Surely that makes him a s*** spy.

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:54.160
Only for one of those sides.

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:56.040
♪ Dun dun dun! ♪

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:58.000
So during the early days of World War 2

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:01.680
he decided he had to make a contribution for
the good of humanity.

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:03.800
And he went to the British who said…

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:04.720
Alright.

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:05.560
Nah.

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:07.000
Have a point, Gary.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:08.640
Sorry, British. "...No."

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:14.480
Three different times they turned him down
as a spy, so what did he do instead?

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:17.680
Oh, did he just go in and start feeding us
information without …

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:19.200
without us asking him to?

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:22.240
"That was dreadfully rude of him
but jolly useful."

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:26.440
He's only helped the British without asking,
but not by talking to them.

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:27.760
-Oh, did he talk…
-I was…

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:30.240
-Chris.
-Guz... ah...

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:34.200
Posed as a British spy and fed false
information to the Germans.

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:36.760
Spot on, absolutely right, he …

00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:41.480
he created an identity as a fanatically
pro-Nazi Spanish government official,

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:44.000
who could travel to London on business

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:47.000
and he just started
sending false reports to the Germans.

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:49.520
"Hello, I'm a fanatical Nazi. (I'm not.)"

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:52.440
Which meant everyone thought he was a spy...

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:54.360
but he just wasn't.

00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:57.240
Yes, have a point.

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:03.920
So the Germans accepted him and said,
"Okay, you're a spy now."

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:08.520
They gave him equipment, they gave him a bit
of money and he moved to Lisbon in Portugal.

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:11.200
Can he see the coast, can he see what ships
are going round Portugal?

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:13.680
Look at England from afar!

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:17.960
"I'd go over there to spy directly
but the weather, really."

00:04:17.960 --> 00:04:21.440
His instructions were move to Britain
and recruit a network of British agents.

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:23.520
Did he pretend he was in Britain
when he was actually in Portugal?

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:24.600
Yes.

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:29.240
-Clever bastard.
-He's a really good spy,

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:33.000
He's such a good spy that he isn't a spy
and everyone thought he was a spy.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:35.800
And then he told them that he was spying
and he wasn't doing the spying.

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:38.840
How is he actually sending a report to them?
Because he is sending reports in.

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:42.200
Is he sending them to London who will then
direct it via London or something,

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:43.640
so they're coming from the right route?

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:47.000
He's just throwing them over the wall
and running away?

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:49.320
No, he's staying in Lisbon for this.

00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:50.560
Pretending?

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:52.480
Yes.

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:53.640
Did he just lie?

00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:54.840
Yes.

00:04:55.840 --> 00:04:58.120
What might he have used
as a basis for that?

00:04:58.440 --> 00:05:02.120
Because remember, he's just sitting in Lisbon
sending completely false reports about Britain.

00:05:02.120 --> 00:05:03.360
Was he listening to British radio?

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:05.720
Erm, newsreel reports in cinemas.

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:09.680
But, yeah, he's looking at the news,
and using a tourist guide to Britain.

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:14.680
Which, correct me if I'm wrong:
the Nazis could watch the news as well?

00:05:15.320 --> 00:05:17.920
Yes. But they still believed him.

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:22.920
Because they saw it on the news, they thought
that what he was saying was correct,

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:25.280
because he'd seen it on the news.

00:05:26.440 --> 00:05:28.600
He was confirming the British propaganda

00:05:29.560 --> 00:05:30.680
but by…

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:34.680
But he was, if he's dressed up as a spy saying,
"Yes, I know these things."

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:37.800
And then the news says those things because
he's already seen the news and they think he's…

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:39.080
Yeah, he's right.

00:05:39.080 --> 00:05:41.520
Yes, you're absolutely right,
that's pretty much what happened.

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:45.600
There was one slight problem, and he's putting
expense reports in, by the way here.

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:48.480
-He's getting reimbursed for this.
-He's living the dream now, now I'm with him.

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:50.640
What's the slight problem
with his expense report?

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:54.080
Oh, was Portuguese currency decimals,
so he couldn't forge the receipts?

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:55.040
Yes.

00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:59.360
Because it's really hard to convert
decimal to an imperial currency system

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:01.200
because it's pegged completely differently.

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:04.240
Yeah, more than that, he didn't know
how pounds, shillings and pence worked.

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:07.040
Because he'd never been there.

00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:10.160
Yes, so he's making mistakes
but the reports are still credible,

00:06:10.520 --> 00:06:12.720
so credible that what happens?

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:16.240
Do we spend a lot of time trying to find him
and couldn't because he's in Lisbon?

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:22.680
MI5 launched a full scale spy hunt
after they intercepted his reports.

00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:25.280
How can they intercept his reports
when they're coming from Portugal?

00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:28.280
Because he's probably radioing them
or Morse coding them across

00:06:28.280 --> 00:06:29.960
and they'll be cracking that, won't they?

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:32.680
Yeah, through the Ultra programme, yes,
you're absolutely right.

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:37.040
So they did eventually move him to Britain
and they gave him a code name.

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:39.160
Was it a bit of a shock for him
to actually move to Britain?

00:06:39.160 --> 00:06:43.160
He was given a code name
for a very British drink.

00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:44.800
Crème de menthe.

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:49.040
"Tea."

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:50.080
Bovril.

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:51.200
Oh!

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:55.440
And he's no idea, they're going around,
"What's your code name?"

00:06:55.440 --> 00:06:57.080
-"Agent Bovril."
-"Midnight."

00:06:57.080 --> 00:06:58.360
What's yours? "Bovril."

00:07:00.200 --> 00:07:02.720
But they did change his code name later to…

00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:03.960
-Marmite.
-Marmite.

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:07.440
Code name Garbo.

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:09.440
Nice. So from Bovril to Garbo.

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:10.200
Yes.

00:07:10.200 --> 00:07:12.280
An autobiography there for everybody.

00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:13.360
So what was he actually sending?

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:15.720
We've got a mixture of three things here,
complete fiction,

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:17.400
what's the other two categories?

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:18.880
-Newspaper clippings.
-Football scores.

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:22.560
Genuine information of little military value,
but you know what, football scores is fine,

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:23.640
I'll give you a point.

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:26.080
Was he sending stuff that …
well, okay, for want of a better phrase,

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:29.400
was he leaving them breadcrumbs of things
he thought might be happening soon,

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:33.120
so they would keep drip-feeding very small
amounts in to keep them on the hook?

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:37.000
Ooh! You're close,
but there is one thing they did.

00:07:37.000 --> 00:07:39.720
He … the first bit of this
is 'valuable military intelligence'.

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:44.480
He did actually send proper intelligence to
the Germans, there's just one catch with it.

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:46.200
It was harmless,
so it was out of date or late?

00:07:46.200 --> 00:07:48.040
Yes, artificially delayed.

00:07:48.040 --> 00:07:49.000
Ah!

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:52.760
So he sent perfectly accurate data,
it was postmarked in time.

00:07:52.760 --> 00:07:53.920
Ah! And they're going to say,

00:07:53.920 --> 00:07:56.320
"If we had only read this quicker,
this would have…

00:07:56.320 --> 00:07:59.720
"We'd intercept this because we read this
about three days later than we could have.

00:08:00.040 --> 00:08:03.520
"And if only we had more staff reading this
stuff, we would have got there quicker."

00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:04.960
So they employed more staff, yeah?

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:08.600
I quote, 'We are sorry they arrived too late,
but your last reports were magnificent.'

00:08:10.200 --> 00:08:12.800
The Germans wanted quicker reports
and better encryption.

00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:14.280
They didn't get that.

00:08:14.280 --> 00:08:17.080
Well, they did, what did they send him?

00:08:17.080 --> 00:08:18.120
-An Enigma machine.
-Yes.

00:08:18.120 --> 00:08:20.320
No! No!

00:08:21.560 --> 00:08:24.120
You knobheads!

00:08:25.640 --> 00:08:30.040
It's not an Enigma machine but it's the
strongest code book that the Germans had,

00:08:30.040 --> 00:08:32.400
-was promptly sent to him.
-F***ing hell!

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:34.720
He must have thought it was bleeding Christmas
when that happened.

00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:37.040
Straight down Bletchley on his bike,
"Got one!"

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:41.720
What was Operation Fortitude in 1944?

00:08:41.720 --> 00:08:43.120
A good hearty breakfast.

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:46.840
Did it come after Operation 39itude?

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:49.480
Oh!

00:08:53.000 --> 00:08:54.120
No.

00:08:54.120 --> 00:08:57.460
But it came immediately before
another famous operation.

00:08:57.460 --> 00:08:58.740
-41itude!
-41itude!

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:03.040
I set that one up, didn't I?

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:06.920
-Overlord.
-Yes, which was?

00:09:06.920 --> 00:09:08.080
-D-Day.
-Yes.

00:09:08.080 --> 00:09:11.040
So immediately before Operation Overlord
was the D-Day landings.

00:09:11.040 --> 00:09:13.480
Operation Fortitude was designed to do what?

00:09:13.480 --> 00:09:15.480
This isn't the thing with the inflatable tanks,
is it?

00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:17.240
Yes, it is, and in fact...

00:09:17.240 --> 00:09:19.640
Mystery Biscuits.

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:24.320
They'd made some tank shaped balloons...

00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:26.680
balloon shaped tanks.

00:09:27.160 --> 00:09:29.480
-Tank shaped balloons, right the first time.
-I've heard of this before.

00:09:29.480 --> 00:09:31.760
Yeah, and they positioned them strategically

00:09:31.760 --> 00:09:34.560
in exactly the place the Germans thought
they would be positioned.

00:09:34.560 --> 00:09:38.920
And for some reason,
I've never quite worked out, that was enough.

00:09:38.920 --> 00:09:41.240
Despite the fact I'm almost certain one of them

00:09:41.240 --> 00:09:44.120
will probably have got ended up filled with
helium, it might have floated upwards.

00:09:44.120 --> 00:09:47.880
I like the idea that they're not pumped up
enough and they just got really limp guns.

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:52.520
So they accept he's going to send some
genuine information.

00:09:52.520 --> 00:09:53.880
And he's got the German …

00:09:53.880 --> 00:09:56.440
he's got the German radio operators
listening in all night.

00:09:57.120 --> 00:09:59.240
And he sends them, at 3am, some reasonable,

00:09:59.240 --> 00:10:02.760
maybe slightly too late details of a bit of
what's going on.

00:10:02.760 --> 00:10:04.000
"There's an invasion happening, you know!"

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:05.560
-What happened?
-The Germans were asleep.

00:10:05.560 --> 00:10:07.280
-Yes.
-Oh!

00:10:07.280 --> 00:10:10.560
Well, we don't know if they were asleep,
they just didn't send a reply until 8am.

00:10:10.560 --> 00:10:12.840
"Sorry, was asleep, had phone on silent",
that kind of thing.

00:10:12.840 --> 00:10:15.960
Yes, so what does he do
because it's now definitely too late?

00:10:15.960 --> 00:10:18.480
Sends an incensed message
that they aren't listening to him.

00:10:18.480 --> 00:10:22.120
Yes. "I cannot accept
excuses or negligence."

00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:24.800
He's bollocking them, I love it.

00:10:24.800 --> 00:10:29.720
And then sends pretty much
the accurate details of the invasion,

00:10:30.200 --> 00:10:32.320
which is happening right now,

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:35.320
and they can't do anything about
because they were too late.

00:10:35.320 --> 00:10:36.400
Oh!

00:10:37.600 --> 00:10:40.200
That, oh, that gives him a free pass.

00:10:40.200 --> 00:10:41.240
Yes, it does.

00:10:41.240 --> 00:10:43.960
Because he's like, "I would have told you
had you been awake at 3:00am."

00:10:43.960 --> 00:10:46.920
The Germans paid Garbo how much,

00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:50.520
over the network of agents,
over 27 fabricated people,

00:10:50.520 --> 00:10:56.000
over years, I've got a number here in US dollars,
the Germans sent straight to MI5?

00:10:56.000 --> 00:10:57.680
Am I allowed to guess in Reichsmarks?

00:10:57.680 --> 00:11:01.520
No. But we're going to do
Price is Right rules here, as usual,

00:11:01.520 --> 00:11:03.160
-closest without going over.
-US dollars?

00:11:03.160 --> 00:11:04.680
US dollars, 1944.

00:11:04.680 --> 00:11:06.040
One million.

00:11:06.040 --> 00:11:09.160
I'm going to say it's about $10.92
because they never actually paid him,

00:11:09.160 --> 00:11:11.080
because he was in the wrong place
at the wrong time.

00:11:11.080 --> 00:11:14.640
Clever, I was going to go for about 600,000.

00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:20.000
Matt is closest without going over ,
340,000 US dollars.

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:21.040
Ooh!

00:11:21.040 --> 00:11:23.280
Went straight from the German Treasury
to the British one.

00:11:23.280 --> 00:11:25.040
They might as well have just like
sent a plane over

00:11:25.040 --> 00:11:27.120
and just bombed us with banknotes,
mightn't they.

00:11:28.280 --> 00:11:30.680
He was presented with some awards for this.

00:11:30.680 --> 00:11:33.640
Best spy and also controversially, worst spy.

00:11:35.160 --> 00:11:36.680
A German military award.

00:11:36.680 --> 00:11:38.640
-You said the Iron Cross.
-I was hoping you'd forgotten that.

00:11:38.640 --> 00:11:40.080
That must have been awkward turning up
for your…

00:11:40.080 --> 00:11:41.760
because you said a George Cross
or something, didn't you?

00:11:41.760 --> 00:11:44.200
-MBE, he's still wearing the MBE.
-With the Iron Cross.

00:11:44.200 --> 00:11:45.000
Yes.

00:11:45.000 --> 00:11:48.360
A suspiciously Iron Cross shaped mark
on where his breast pocket was.

00:11:48.360 --> 00:11:51.040
S***, did he turn up in Germany
to receive awards?

00:11:51.040 --> 00:11:53.080
No, it was awarded by radio,

00:11:53.680 --> 00:11:56.920
and then he received the physical medal
from one of his German handlers

00:11:56.920 --> 00:11:58.320
after the war had ended.

00:11:58.320 --> 00:12:00.160
But if he didn't like the institution

00:12:00.160 --> 00:12:02.840
and he had all of these awards from two
different institutions,

00:12:02.840 --> 00:12:04.720
surely he f***ing hated that.

00:12:05.040 --> 00:12:06.360
Yes.

00:12:06.360 --> 00:12:08.360
But why did he have to accept it anyway?

00:12:08.360 --> 00:12:11.400
Because otherwise it would blow his cover
and he'd get killed by some mysterious agents,

00:12:11.400 --> 00:12:13.240
they probably thought still existed
at the time.

00:12:13.240 --> 00:12:16.040
Yes. He feared reprisals after the war.

00:12:16.040 --> 00:12:18.960
Because we all assume, like,
neatly the war ended in '45.

00:12:18.960 --> 00:12:21.800
They weren't very sure the war was
actually over for quite a long time.

00:12:21.800 --> 00:12:25.320
Because he kind of thought,
"They'll be back", like they are in films.

00:12:25.320 --> 00:12:27.440
Like there's a secret cache of Nazis that...

00:12:27.440 --> 00:12:28.440
After the credits.

00:12:28.440 --> 00:12:29.480
Yeah.

00:12:29.480 --> 00:12:33.320
A single Nazi helmet pops up
out of the ground, ♪ Dun dun dun dun ♪

00:12:33.320 --> 00:12:36.480
Well, you know,
70 years later they're back. Anyway…

00:12:36.480 --> 00:12:37.680
Ooh!

00:12:39.560 --> 00:12:40.440
So what did he do?

00:12:40.440 --> 00:12:42.480
Moved to America and lived a quiet life.

00:12:42.480 --> 00:12:46.040
You know what, I'll give you that, he …
he went to Angola.

00:12:46.040 --> 00:12:47.240
That's not America!

00:12:48.720 --> 00:12:50.400
And then went to Venezuela.

00:12:50.400 --> 00:12:52.160
Like the Nazis did.

00:12:52.160 --> 00:12:55.720
-So if you say America, Venezuela is sort of -
-It's South America.

00:12:55.720 --> 00:12:57.920
-I'll give you the point.
-It's an America.

00:12:57.920 --> 00:12:59.960
-He also did something else.
-Faked his own death.

00:12:59.960 --> 00:13:04.120
Yes, he did, he faked his own death
from malaria in 1949.

00:13:04.120 --> 00:13:05.280
And then...

00:13:06.040 --> 00:13:09.520
changed his identity,
moved to Venezuela and lived doing what?

00:13:09.520 --> 00:13:10.560
Hard drugs.

00:13:10.560 --> 00:13:12.160
Printing expense forms.

00:13:12.160 --> 00:13:13.600
Money laundering.

00:13:13.600 --> 00:13:16.440
A bookshop. A bookshop and gift shop.

00:13:16.440 --> 00:13:18.280
"The books are full of spies."

00:13:19.160 --> 00:13:25.000
In 1971, British politician, Rupert Allason is
trying to track down Garbo.

00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:26.160
Is this for his MBE?

00:13:26.160 --> 00:13:28.280
-No, he's got the MBE.
-He's already got that?

00:13:28.280 --> 00:13:30.680
But no one knows his real name.

00:13:31.120 --> 00:13:32.960
Does everyone just know him as Bovril or Garbo?

00:13:32.960 --> 00:13:34.040
Yes, essentially.

00:13:34.040 --> 00:13:36.000
I like the name as a full name, Bovrila Garbo.

00:13:37.160 --> 00:13:41.200
Eventually Allason tracks down someone who
supplied his full name

00:13:41.200 --> 00:13:43.280
and knew roughly where he was.

00:13:43.280 --> 00:13:45.480
So what does Allason then do?

00:13:45.480 --> 00:13:50.440
Has he gone through every Juan Garcia in
Venezuela but there are loads of them?

00:13:50.440 --> 00:13:52.280
You know, that's close enough,
I'll give you the point.

00:13:52.280 --> 00:13:55.800
He went through every J Garcia in the Barcelona
phone book

00:13:57.520 --> 00:13:59.760
and eventually got in touch with his nephew.

00:14:00.360 --> 00:14:02.080
-In Barcelona?
-In Barcelona.

00:14:02.080 --> 00:14:03.120
That's not Venezuela.

00:14:03.120 --> 00:14:05.120
No, because no one knew where he'd gone.

00:14:05.120 --> 00:14:09.600
Oh, of course, yeah, we know that, so yeah,
"Hello was your… was anyone you know a spy?"

00:14:09.600 --> 00:14:11.360
Yes, that's basically what happened.

00:14:11.360 --> 00:14:14.200
And this person said yes and they believed him.

00:14:15.040 --> 00:14:17.360
That's exactly the thing a spy wouldn't do.

00:14:19.000 --> 00:14:20.800
And you make an excellent point there.

00:14:20.800 --> 00:14:25.200
I was going to say that Pujol travelled to
London and it says here,

00:14:25.200 --> 00:14:27.600
"was received by Prince Philip
at Buckingham Palace".

00:14:27.600 --> 00:14:29.800
But now you've said that, who knows who it was?

00:14:29.800 --> 00:14:31.480
Because Prince Philip never met him.

00:14:31.480 --> 00:14:34.440
Well, you say that, he then went to the
Special Forces Club and was reunited

00:14:34.440 --> 00:14:35.800
with a group of
his former colleagues.

00:14:35.800 --> 00:14:39.160
He told them he'd had plastic surgery while he
was in Venezuela.

00:14:39.160 --> 00:14:41.720
And so looked and sounded different
because was…

00:14:41.720 --> 00:14:43.360
"Oh, Bovril, up to your old tricks."

00:14:43.360 --> 00:14:44.360
"Yeah, Bovril."

00:14:44.360 --> 00:14:47.520
You do know that plastic surgery doesn't work
like it does in the Bond movies, right?

00:14:47.520 --> 00:14:48.960
Shut up.

00:14:48.960 --> 00:14:52.920
Yes, they called every single J Garcia in the
Barcelona phone book.

00:14:52.920 --> 00:14:58.320
They found his nephew, his nephew put the
politician in touch with the original spy.

00:14:58.320 --> 00:15:03.080
And he came over and met Prince Philip,
toured the beaches of Normandy.

00:15:03.080 --> 00:15:07.480
And eventually died in Caracas in 1988 and is
essentially

00:15:07.800 --> 00:15:10.720
the greatest spy that Britain
nearly didn't have.

00:15:12.920 --> 00:15:15.440
And when he came over he brought a big stack
of expense forms

00:15:15.440 --> 00:15:17.080
for all his staff back in the office.

00:15:18.440 --> 00:15:20.760
So at the end of the show, congratulations,
Chris you win this one.

00:15:25.320 --> 00:15:28.480
You win a trip to an
'all you can eat' garlic restaurant.

00:15:28.480 --> 00:15:30.240
Aioli you can eat?

00:15:30.920 --> 00:15:32.200
Buffet the Vampire Slayer.

00:15:32.200 --> 00:15:33.280
Oh!

00:15:36.120 --> 00:15:38.360
With that, we thank you to
Chris Joel,

00:15:38.760 --> 00:15:40.320
to Gary Brannan,

00:15:40.320 --> 00:15:41.720
to Matt Gray.

00:15:41.720 --> 00:15:43.520
I've been Tom Scott,
we'll see you next time.

